This proposes skl, a utility in src/skl. You asked for it in "Re:
[RFC] how to organize experiments in claude?" [1]. It is a design to
argue with, and I have written no code yet. Anything marked [T] I ran
here with Claude Code 2.1.296.

* Why

We want to try different ways of working, such as build-by-commit and
build-by-spec, on real asks. A session must have the skill we chose
and nothing else. It must not be able to pick the other variant, or
even know that it exists.

The choice is ours, and it is made before the model starts. Hiding
skills with claude flags, like skillOverrides or disallowedTools,
puts that choice in claude's config, where it is a deny list. Every
new variant is live until someone remembers to hide it. skl moves the
choice outside claude. We name what we want, skl builds a session that
contains only that, and claude is just the thing it runs. That is "Own
the loop" applied to configuration and not only to output.

* Where things live

- src/claude-plugins/<plugin>/ holds reusable skills, the ones meant
  for use outside this repo. Each is a normal Claude Code plugin:
  .claude-plugin/plugin.json plus skills/<skill>/SKILL.md. One plugin
  can hold several skills, e.g. src/claude-plugins/build/ with
  skills/by-commit and skills/by-spec.
- .claude/skills/ keeps only skills about this repo itself.
- src/skl/ holds the tool: skl, skl.1, skl_test, Kyuafile, and a
  Justfile and pkg recipe like the ones in src/lab-process-mail.
- Installing skl installs every plugin under src/claude-plugins to
  ${PREFIX}/share/skl/plugins/<plugin>/. Sessions load that copy and
  never the working tree, as in "Lab repo shouldn't operate on itself"
  [2]. Editing a skill takes effect at the next install, not in
  sessions already running.

* Interface

    skl p [-p plugin[:skill]]... [-C dir] [-- claude-args...] < prompt
    skl ls [plugin]

skl p runs one prompt. It reads the prompt from stdin and runs claude
-p in dir, the current directory by default, with exactly the selected
skills loaded:

- "-p build" loads the whole build plugin.
- "-p build:by-commit" loads only that skill from it.
- -p repeats. With no -p, no plugin skills are loaded.
- Anything after -- goes to claude unchanged, e.g. --permission-mode.

skl ls lists the installed plugins, or one plugin's skills. Its output
has one name per line, so a script can check a selector before using
it.

skl never reads claude's output. Its stdout and stderr are claude's,
and its exit status is claude's, except that skl exits 64 for a bad
usage and 66 for a selector that names no installed plugin or skill.
A caller that needs a result checks the side effect, just as
lab-process-mail checks the inbox today.

* How selection works

claude only loads what is in a --plugin-dir. So for each run, skl
builds a temporary directory with one plugin per selected name. Each
has a copy of the installed plugin.json and a symlink for each chosen
skill. It passes each one with --plugin-dir and removes the directory
on exit. A skill that was not selected is not in any directory claude
reads, so it cannot be loaded. I tested both halves of this:

- [T] With the whole build plugin passed, both build:by-commit and
  build:by-spec are listed. With a plugin dir holding only a symlink
  to by-commit, only build:by-commit is listed.
- [T] skillOverrides set to "off" did not hide a plugin skill. I tried
  both "build:by-spec" and "by-spec" as the key, and both stayed
  listed. So the override approach does not work for skills inside
  plugins. It would only work for skills under .claude/skills.

The skill keeps its plugin's namespace, so by-commit is still invoked
as build:by-commit. A prompt that should work with either variant
cannot name the skill directly. It has to say "use the build skill
you have" or something similar. See open question 3.

* What skl does not control

The selected plugins are added to what claude already loads, which is
its built-in skills and anything from user and project settings.

- [T] --setting-sources user dropped a skill that a default session in
  this workspace lists from project scope. I did not trace where that
  skill came from. skl could pass --setting-sources user by default,
  but then the project's permissions in .claude/settings.json would be
  dropped too. See open question 2.
- Built-in skills such as /init and /code-review are always present.
  I found no flag that removes them, and they are not variants we test.

* Commit plan

1. skl: Add skl p to run a prompt with chosen skills

   Adds skl with p and ls, skl.1, and kyua tests. The tests stub
   claude and check the --plugin-dir arguments, the contents of the
   temporary directory, the cleanup, and the exit statuses.

2. skl: Install the plugins under src/claude-plugins

   Adds the Justfile and pkg recipe. The package carries the plugins
   in share/skl/plugins.

3. claude-plugins/build: Add the by-commit and by-spec skills

   by-commit uses the commit-plan rules from "Re: [RFC] how to spec
   work in a way that's readable for humans" [3]. by-spec is built
   from the old spec-report skill.

Changing lab-process-mail to run through skl is left for a separate
series, once skl exists.

* Open questions

1. Is "p" the subcommand name you want, and should "-p" be the
   selector? -p also means "print" to claude, so "skl p -p" might
   mislead a reader who knows claude. "-s" for skill is an option.
2. Should skl p load project and user settings by default, or pass
   --setting-sources user and make you opt in to the rest?
3. Should variants of the same job live in one plugin (build:by-commit,
   build:by-spec) or in separate plugins that share a skill name
   (by-commit:build, by-spec:build)? One plugin is easier to browse.
   Separate plugins let a prompt name "build" without knowing which
   variant is loaded, but then two of them cannot be loaded together.
   I lean to one plugin per job and prompts written to be neutral
   about the variant.
4. Where does skl's own documentation live? skl.1 is sure. Whether
   there is also a README.org depends on "[RFC] documenting monorepos"
   [4], which is still open.

